  "raw_text": "321\n\nfrente, mi cráneo\n \nfrente, tu cráneo\n \nfrente,\n \nsu\n \ncráneo\n\nMihta\n \n–\n \nhand\n \n/ mano\n \nMihti\n \n–\n \nmy hand\n \n/ mi mano\n \nMihtam\n \n–\n \nyour hand\n \n/ tu\n\nmano\n\nAi mihta\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\nhand\n \n/ su mano\n\nMina\n \n–\n \nfoot\n \n/ pie\n \nMini\n \n–\n \nmy foot\n \n/ mi pie\n \nMinam\n \n–\n \nmy foot\n \n/ tu pie\n \nAi mina\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\nfoot\n \n/ su pie\n\nNakra\n \n–\n \neye\n \n/ ojo\n \nNaikra\n \n–\n \nmy eye\n \n/ mi o\njo\n \nNamkra\n \n–\n \nyour eye\n \n/ tu ojo\n \nAi nakra\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\neye\n \n/ su ojo\n\nTâwa\n \n–\n \nhair\n \n/ cabello\n \nT\nâ\niwa\n \n–\n \nmy hair\n \n/ mi\n\ncabello\n\nTâmwa\n \n–\n \nyour hair\n \n/ tu\n\ncabello\n\nAi tâwa\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\nhair\n \n/ su cabello\n\nTwisa\n \n–\n \ntongue\n \n/ lengua\n \nTwisi\n \n–\n \nmy tongue\n \n/ mi\n\nlengua\n\nTwisam\n \n–\n \nyour tongue\n \n/ tu\n\nlengua\n\nAi twisa\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\ntongue\n \n/ su lengua\n\nFor nouns that end in the letter “\na\n”, the “\na\n” is often\n \n(not always)\n \nreplaced with the letter “\ni\n” followed by the\n \nki\n,\n \nkam\n, or\n\nka\n \nending /\n\nPara los sustantivos que terminan en la letra \"\na\n\", la \"\na\n\" a menudo se reemplaza (no siempre) con la letra \"\ni\n\" seguida de\n\nla terminación\n \nki\n,\n \nkam\n \no\n \nka\n\nExamples/Ejemplos:\n\nAisa\n \n–\n \nfather\n \n/ padre\n \nAisiki\n \n–\n \nmy father\n \n/ mi\n\npadre\n\nAisikam\n \n–\n \nfather\n \n/ tu padre\n \nAi aisika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\n\nfather\n \n/ su padre\n\nAlba\n \n–\n \nslave\n \n/ esclavo\n \nAlbiki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nslave\n \n/\n \nmi\n\nesclavo\n\nAlbikam\n \n–\n \nyour\n \nslave\n \n/\n \ntu\n\nesclavo\n\nAi albika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\nslave\n \n/ su esclavo\n\nInska\n \n–\n \nfish\n \n/ pescado\n \nInskiki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nfish\n \n/ mi\n\npescado\n\nInskikam\n \n–\n \nyour fish\n \n/ tu\n\npescado\n\nAi inskika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/\n\ntheir fish\n \n/\n \nsu\n \npescado\n\nKuka\n \n–\n \ngrandmother\n \n/\nabuela\n\nKukiki\n \n–\n \nmy grandmother\n \n/\n\nmi abuela\n\nKukikam\n \n–\n \nyour\n\ngrandmother\n \n/ tu abuela\n\nAi kukika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\n\ngrandmother\n \n/ su abuela\n\nMama\n \n–\n \nmama, mother\n \n/\n\nmama, madre\n\nMamiki\n \n–\n \nmy\n \nmama,\n \nmy\n\nmother\n \n/\n \nmi\n \nmama,\n \nmi\n\nmadre\n\nMamikam\n \n–\n \nyour mama,\n\nyour mother\n \n/ tu mama, tu\n\nmadre\n\nAi mamika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\n\nmama, mother\n \n/ su mama,\n\nsu\n \nmadre\n\nThere are many other irregularities\n \n/\n \nHay muchas otras irregularidades\n:\n\nExamples/Ejemplos:\n\nDaiwan\n \n–\n \nanimal\n \n/ animal\n \nDaiwri\n \n–\n \nmy animal\n \n/ mi\n\nanimal\n\nDaiwram\n \n–\n \nyour animal\n \n/\n\ntu animal\n\nAi daiwra\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\n\nanimal\n \n/ su animal\n\nDiara\n \n–\n \nthing\n \n/ cosa\n \nDuki\n \n–\n \nmy thing\n \n/ mi cosa\n \nDukiam\n \n–\n \nyour thing\n \n/ tu\n\ncosa\n\nAi dukia\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their\n\nthing\n \n/ su cosa\n\nTuktan\n \n–\n \nchild\n \n/ niño\n \nTuktiki\n \n–\n \nmy child\n \n/ mi niño\n \nTuktikam\n \n–\n \nyour child\n \n/ tu\n\nniño\n\nAi tuktika\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their\n\nchild\n \n/ su niño\n\nYul\n \n–\n \ndog\n \n/ perro\n \nYuli\n \n–\n \nmy dog\n \n/ mi perro\n \nYulam\n \n–\n \nyour dog\n \n/ tu\n\nperro\n\nAi yula\n \n–\n \nhis/her/their dog\n\n/ su perro",
